# Suppressions for reachable memory held by OpenSSL's own internal caches
# (RSA blinding state, BN_MONT_CTX Montgomery contexts, encoder and provider
# tables, and similar lazily-computed values populated during RSA key
# generation and signing). tests_parser's stream_tls test group is what
# reaches them here: it drives a real TLS handshake off a certificate
# ctls_ctx_cert_add() generates in process, so the key generation happens
# inside libcrypto rather than in a separate openssl(1) process.
#
# These blocks are allocated through OpenSSL's own CRYPTO_malloc/CRYPTO_zalloc
# wrappers and kept alive for the lifetime of the process by libcrypto's own
# internal static and thread-local state; they are only released by an
# explicit OPENSSL_cleanup() call, which this codebase does not make (calling
# it unconditionally at process exit is not safe in a library meant to be
# embedded in a larger application that may still have other OpenSSL handles
# in use). They are not leaks, matching the same category already documented
# in ld_so_dlopen.supp for glibc's own NSS module loading.
#
# Matched by the allocation's own ORIGIN LIBRARY rather than by a wrapper
# function name. Whether CRYPTO_malloc/CRYPTO_zalloc/CRYPTO_strdup/
# CRYPTO_strndup/BN_MONT_CTX_new/OPENSSL_sk_deep_copy (and others) show up as
# their own frame between malloc/realloc and the rest of the stack, or get
# inlined away entirely, is a choice of however that particular libcrypto.so.3
# was built, not something this codebase controls; the one frame that is
# always present regardless is that the caller of malloc/realloc lives in
# libcrypto.so.3, which is exactly what these rules require. A block libssl
# asks for is covered by the same rules, because libssl allocates through
# CRYPTO_malloc, which lives in libcrypto.
#
# That frame is pinned to position one, immediately above the allocator,
# deliberately: a rule that merely requires a libcrypto frame SOMEWHERE in the
# stack also matches a block THIS PROJECT's own code allocates while running
# underneath libcrypto, which ctls.c does every time OpenSSL invokes its SNI
# or ALPN callback. Such a block is this project's to account for, and these
# rules leave it reported.
{
   openssl_internal_still_reachable_malloc_from_libcrypto
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: reachable
   fun:malloc
   obj:*/libcrypto.so.3
   ...
}
{
   openssl_internal_still_reachable_realloc_from_libcrypto
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: reachable
   fun:realloc
   obj:*/libcrypto.so.3
   ...
}
